{
  "status": "complete",
  "summary": "The strongest pattern in current evidence is a lightweight feedback loop, not a heavy ML pipeline: persist each finding, capture explicit and implicit outcome signals, and recalibrate thresholds by rule/category from observed precision and downstream developer behavior. Qodo’s public docs are the clearest mechanism reference: second-pass self-reflection and score thresholds at generation time, private-wiki persistence for accepted suggestions, monthly mining of accepted patterns into reusable best practices, and impact metrics normalized by code volume. Research and Uber’s production write-up both suggest that explicit thumbs/rating signals are useful but insufficient on their own; the highest-value signals are implementation, dismissal reason, discussion content, repeat-after-dismissal, and revert-after-apply, while recall still requires a benchmark or sampled human audit set. Evidence for the existence of these mechanisms is strong, but public evidence for exact calibration math is thin, and vendor-reported effectiveness numbers should be treated as directional rather than universal.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "Qodo’s documented self-reflection loop is a second-pass re-ranking/filtering stage: the model reviews its own suggestions, scores them, drops obviously bad ones, and can apply a configurable threshold.",
      "source": "https://docs.qodo.ai/qodo-documentation/qodo-merge/pr-agent/core-abilities/self_reflection",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "The page lists a follow-up call, `0-10` scoring, score-0 filtering, and `suggestions_score_threshold`; it was updated 8 days ago."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Qodo already implements a lightweight file-based persistence model: it tracks direct applies and indirect manual implementations, then stores accepted suggestions in a private repo wiki instead of an external database.",
      "source": "https://docs.qodo.ai/qodo-documentation/qodo-merge/learn-more/tracking",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Tracking docs say Apply clicks and new commits are both detected, and accepted suggestions are saved in `.pr_agent_accepted_suggestions` in the repo wiki, 'instead of using an external server or database.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Qodo’s longer-term learning loop is pattern mining from accepted suggestions: accepted items are analyzed monthly and turned into repository-specific best practices that influence future reviews.",
      "source": "https://docs.qodo.ai/qodo-documentation/qodo-merge/features/best-practices",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Best Practices docs say accepted patterns are analyzed 'once a month' into `.pr_agent_auto_best_practices`, then reused in later reviews with a `Learned best practice` label."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Qodo exposes impact metrics that are useful for threshold calibration, including implemented suggestions per 1K lines, category effectiveness, and score distribution for implemented suggestions.",
      "source": "https://docs.qodo.ai/qodo-documentation/qodo-merge/learn-more/impact-evaluation",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Impact Evaluation documents `Qodo Impacts per 1K Lines`, category-level effectiveness, and score distribution for implemented suggestions, all updated 2 months ago."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Qodo supports thumbs-up/down as a developer feedback signal, but its own docs say that signal is only for statistics and does not directly change future model feedback.",
      "source": "https://docs.qodo.ai/qodo-documentation/qodo-merge/pr-agent/tools/index/improve",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "The `allow_thumbs_up_down` config is described as 'for statistics tracking' and 'will not affect future feedback from the AI model.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "GitHub Copilot exposes the core feedback signals needed for a local outcome tracker: accept/implement suggestion, resolve/hide, thumbs up/down with optional negative reason, and re-review; however, dismissed findings may reappear on re-review.",
      "source": "https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/use-copilot-agents/request-a-code-review/use-code-review",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "GitHub docs say comments can be resolved and hidden, thumbs-down can include a reason, and re-review 'may repeat the same comments' even after resolve/downvote."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Explicit ratings are too sparse to be the main learning signal; conversation text and action rate are richer and more available outcome signals.",
      "source": "https://assets.amazon.science/e7/c5/a2912e944e758962bb1668e6aafe/understanding-developer-analyzer-interactions-in-code-reviews.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "The ASE 2024 paper reports direct ratings in '<5%' of cases, while over 15% of CodeGuru comments receive at least one reply, rising to about 30% for the top 100 rules."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Outcome schemas should separate finding validity from remediation acceptance, because developer responses often accept the issue but reject or misunderstand the proposed fix.",
      "source": "https://assets.amazon.science/e7/c5/a2912e944e758962bb1668e6aafe/understanding-developer-analyzer-interactions-in-code-reviews.pdf",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "The label set includes `Accept both finding and remediation`, `Accept finding but reject remediation`, `Reject finding in current context`, and `Tool is wrong`."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Human adoption limits are trust and context, not just detection quality; overly detailed AI review output can increase reviewer cognitive load.",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02092",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "The CHASE 2025 study with 20 interviewees says adoption is constrained by 'trust and lack of context' and that cognitive load can be higher with excessive LLM detail."
    },
    {
      "claim": "For offline measurement of code review quality, reference-free metrics are maturing; CRScore aligns better with human judgment than older reference-based metrics.",
      "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.19801",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "CRScore reports 0.54 Spearman alignment with human judgment and releases 2.9k human-annotated review-quality scores."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The clearest public example of threshold calibration from outcomes is Uber’s uReview: confidence thresholds are tuned per assistant/language/category from feedback and evaluation, production usefulness is tracked automatically, and recall is measured on a labeled benchmark rather than from telemetry alone.",
      "source": "https://www.uber.com/en-TR/blog/ureview/",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "Uber says thresholds are set per assistant, language, and category; usefulness stays above 75%; about 65% of posted comments are addressed; precision/recall/F1 come from annotated commits. This is vendor-authored evidence."
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "code_sample",
      "path": "sqlite_schema_v1",
      "description": "Inferred minimal SQLite schema for tracking findings, signals, and final outcomes without a heavy database.",
      "content": "CREATE TABLE findings (\\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\\n  repo TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  pr_number INTEGER NOT NULL,\\n  head_sha TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  tool TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  model TEXT,\\n  rule_id TEXT,\\n  category TEXT,\\n  language TEXT,\\n  file_path TEXT,\\n  line_start INTEGER,\\n  fingerprint TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  raw_severity INTEGER,\\n  raw_score REAL,\\n  created_at TEXT NOT NULL\\n);\\n\\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_findings_unique\\n  ON findings(repo, pr_number, head_sha, fingerprint);\\n\\nCREATE TABLE signals (\\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\\n  finding_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES findings(id),\\n  signal_type TEXT NOT NULL,      -- applied|resolved|dismissed|thumbs_up|thumbs_down|repeated|reverted\\n  signal_value TEXT NOT NULL,     -- true|false or reason code\\n  actor TEXT,                     -- author|reviewer|bot|system\\n  observed_at TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  metadata_json TEXT\\n);\\n\\nCREATE TABLE verdicts (\\n  finding_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES findings(id),\\n  final_outcome TEXT NOT NULL,    -- accepted|rejected|false_positive|duplicate|unknown\\n  finding_validity TEXT,          -- valid|invalid|unclear\\n  remediation_status TEXT,        -- implemented|implemented_alternative|rejected_fix|not_applicable\\n  reviewer_confidence REAL,\\n  decided_at TEXT NOT NULL,\\n  notes TEXT\\n);"
    },
    {
      "type": "data",
      "path": "finding_events_ndjson_v1",
      "description": "Inferred file-based alternative to SQLite; one JSON event per line so the same loop can run from git-tracked files.",
      "content": "{\"finding_id\":\"f_001\",\"repo\":\"org/repo\",\"pr\":417,\"tool\":\"ai-review\",\"rule_id\":\"correctness:null-check\",\"category\":\"correctness\",\"raw_severity\":2,\"raw_score\":8.0,\"fingerprint\":\"src/a.py:42:null-check\",\"signal\":\"created\",\"ts\":\"2026-03-25T17:00:00Z\"}\\n{\"finding_id\":\"f_001\",\"signal\":\"dismissed\",\"reason\":\"not_relevant_in_context\",\"actor\":\"author\",\"ts\":\"2026-03-25T17:04:00Z\"}\\n{\"finding_id\":\"f_001\",\"signal\":\"re_review_repeat\",\"actor\":\"bot\",\"ts\":\"2026-03-25T17:09:00Z\"}\\n{\"finding_id\":\"f_002\",\"signal\":\"applied\",\"actor\":\"author\",\"ts\":\"2026-03-25T17:10:00Z\"}\\n{\"finding_id\":\"f_002\",\"signal\":\"reverted\",\"actor\":\"system\",\"ts\":\"2026-03-27T09:30:00Z\"}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code_sample",
      "path": "threshold_updater_v1",
      "description": "Inferred conservative calibration loop combining production precision/action signals with benchmark recall.",
      "content": "# precision = confirmed_valid / (confirmed_valid + false_positive)\\n# action_rate = implemented / surfaced\\n# recall must come from a golden benchmark or sampled human audits\\n\\nalpha = defaultdict(lambda: 2.0)\\nbeta = defaultdict(lambda: 1.0)\\n\\nfor v in verdicts:\\n    bucket = (v.rule_id or v.category, v.language)\\n    if v.final_outcome == 'accepted':\\n        alpha[bucket] += 1\\n    elif v.final_outcome == 'false_positive':\\n        beta[bucket] += 1\\n    elif v.final_outcome == 'rejected':\\n        beta[bucket] += 0.5  # ambiguous: penalize less than explicit false positive\\n\\nfor bucket in buckets_with_enough_samples(min_n=20):\\n    precision_posterior = alpha[bucket] / (alpha[bucket] + beta[bucket])\\n    if precision_posterior < 0.70 or revert_rate(bucket) > 0.15 or repeat_after_dismissal(bucket) > 0.10:\\n        raise_threshold(bucket)\\n    elif precision_posterior > 0.88 and benchmark_recall(bucket) < 0.60:\\n        lower_threshold(bucket)\\n    else:\\n        keep_threshold(bucket)"
    }
  ],
  "gaps": [
    "I did not find a public Qodo/CodiumAI source that describes an explicit Bayesian or heuristic formula for automatically raising or lowering thresholds by rule/category; the docs expose the signals and knobs, but not the calibration math.",
    "Qodo docs state that accepted suggestions can feed future model training, but I did not find public detail on data retention, selection criteria, or whether learning is per-repo, per-tenant, or global.",
    "Neither Qodo nor GitHub docs define a canonical mapping from `dismissed/rejected` to `false_positive` versus `valid but not worth fixing`; that taxonomy has to be local and explicit.",
    "Recall is not recoverable from PR telemetry alone; you still need a benchmark set or periodic sampled audits to estimate missed findings."
  ],
  "escalations": [
    "Keep `finding_validity` separate from `remediation_status`; collapsing them into one label will corrupt calibration because 'valid finding, wrong fix' is common.",
    "Do not auto-tune thresholds on tiny samples; use a minimum floor such as 20-30 adjudicated findings per rule/category/language bucket.",
    "Track `revert_after_apply` and `repeat_after_dismissal` from day one; otherwise precision will look better than the real developer experience.",
    "Treat Qodo and Uber effectiveness numbers as vendor-biased operational evidence, useful for mechanism design but not as universal baselines."
  ]
}
